<% @title = "Amber's Way — Amber Framework" %>
<% @meta = "The principles behind Amber's complete defaults, clear responsibilities, locally served assets, and honest preview boundaries." %>

<header class="page-hero amber-way-hero">
  <div class="site-container page-hero-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">A framework with a point of view</p>
      <h1>Learn Amber's Way.</h1>
      <p>Own the path from source to browser. Make the default complete. Give every responsibility a clear home. Keep the machinery understandable.</p>
    </div>
    <img class="guide-amber" src="/assets/characters/amber-chibi.webp" width="640" height="640" alt="Chibi Amber holding a laptop and pointing toward the guide">
  </div>
</header>

<main>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="site-container principles-grid">
      <article class="principle-card reveal" id="own-the-path">
        <span>01</span><h2>Own the path.</h2>
        <p>Amber’s essential presentation layer is local HTML, CSS, JavaScript, fonts, and images. A first page does not depend on a third-party CDN staying available or respecting the visitor’s privacy.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="principle-card reveal" id="complete-default">
        <span>02</span><h2>Complete the default.</h2>
        <p><code>amber new my_app</code> creates the web application by default. The path from install to a running page should be coherent before optional application types enter the conversation.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="principle-card reveal" id="clear-responsibilities">
        <span>03</span><h2>Name the responsibility.</h2>
        <p>Framework, records, files, and background work behave differently. Amber, Grant, and Gemma make those boundaries memorable without pretending they are the same release artifact.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="principle-card reveal" id="small-interactions">
        <span>04</span><h2>Keep interaction legible.</h2>
        <p>Use motion to explain progress and state—not to hide it. The terminal demo, browser reveal, cards, and crystal field are small vanilla-JavaScript enhancements over usable static content.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="principle-card reveal" id="honest-boundaries">
        <span>05</span><h2>Label the boundary.</h2>
        <p>Web is the default V2 beta path. Native is a preview. Grant and Gemma evolve independently. The documentation should say exactly which promise a user is evaluating.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="principle-card reveal" id="business-first">
        <span>06</span><h2>Build the business.</h2>
        <p>The framework exists to help one developer move from idea to MVP to a maintainable application. Convention is valuable when it preserves attention for the business problem.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>

